Copyright 2026 Chinnybest321. All rights reserved.

"Tummo" means everything in this repository: the smart contract, the web
app, the artwork (including the pet drawings), the simulation and the docs.

1. What you may do

   a. Read. The repository is public so anyone can read the code and judge
      how Tummo works. GitHub's Terms of Service also let GitHub users view
      and fork public repositories on GitHub. Neither of these grants any
      other right.

   b. Judge. The organizers and judges of NEAR HACKATHON may download,
      build, run and test Tummo to evaluate it. This permission lasts until
      judging and prize payouts for NEAR HACKATHON are complete.

   c. Show. NEAR HACKATHON and NEAR Foundation may use the name "Tummo",
      screenshots, video and a short description of the project to present
      hackathon entries and results.

   d. Play. Anyone may use the live app and interact with the deployed
      contract as a player.

2. What needs permission

   Anything not listed above needs Chinnybest321's written consent first.
   That includes copying, modifying, redeploying, reusing in another
   project, republishing, distributing, sublicensing and selling Tummo or
   any part of it, except where the law allows it without permission.

3. Name and artwork

   The name "Tummo", the logo and the pet artwork may not be used to
   identify any other project, token or product.

4. Testnet, no value, no advice

   Tummo runs on NEAR testnet. Testnet NEAR and TUMMO have no monetary
   value. Nothing in this repository or the app is financial, investment or
   legal advice.

5. No warranty

   Tummo is provided "as is", without any warranty, express or implied,
   including fitness for a particular purpose and absence of bugs. Smart
   contracts can fail. The deployed contract is locked and cannot be fixed.
   You use it at your own risk.

6. No liability

   To the fullest extent the law allows, Chinnybest321 is not liable for any
   loss or damage arising from using, running or relying on Tummo,
   including lost funds or tokens.

7. Dependencies

   Open-source packages that Tummo uses are covered by their own licenses,
   not this one.
